5) Project dir & files meaning

	/src : find the Angular app files	
		index.html  - is the app's top level HTML template.
		styles.css  - is the app's top level style sheet.
		main.ts     - is where the app starts running.
		favicon.ico - is the app's icon, just as you would find in any web site.
	
	/app : find the Angular app's component files 
		app.ts - Is the source file that describes the app-root component
				 This is the top-level Angular component in the app
		
		app.css - is the style sheet for this component.

	
	angular.json - 
				The angular.json file is the primary configuration file for the Angular CLI (Command Line Interface) within an Angular workspace.
				It acts as a central control room that defines the structure and default settings for all projects (applications and libraries) in the workspace. 	
				
				It is used for configuring: 
				
					Workspace-wide settings: 
						Top-level properties define where new projects are created (newProjectRoot) and the schema version used for validation (version, $schema).
						
					Project-specific options: 
						It contains a projects section where each application or library has its own set of configurations.
						
					Build process: 
						It defines how the application should be compiled and bundled, including options for production optimization (minification, tree-shaking, source maps), output paths, asset management, and style preprocessors (e.g., Sass).
						
					Development server: 
						Configurations for the ng serve command, such as the port to listen on, proxy settings, and whether to use HTTPS or live reload.
						
					Testing: 
						Settings for unit tests (using Karma) and end-to-end tests.
						
					Linting: 
						Configuration for linting rules to maintain consistent code quality.
						
					Internationalization (i18n): 
						Options for extracting translation files.
						
					Schematics defaults: 
						Allows customization of the default settings when generating new components, services, etc., using ng generate.
						
					Asset and style management: 
						Specifies which global style sheets, scripts, and static asset files (like images or db.json files for mock APIs) should be included in the build.
						
					Environment-specific configurations: 
						The configurations section allows developers to define different settings (e.g., file replacements, optimization levels) for various environments like development, staging, or production. 
						
					In summary, angular.json is essential for the Angular CLI to know how to build, serve, test, and manage the different parts of an Angular project effectively. 	
				
6) How to create new app from CLI?
	> ng new my-app
	

Workspaces and project files ? 
 
The "ng new" command creates an Angular workspace folder and generates a new application inside it. 
A workspace can contain multiple applications and libraries. 
The initial application created by the "ng new" command is at the root directory of the workspace. In this case "my-app" is the root directory.
When you generate an additional application or library in an existing workspace, it goes into a projects/ subfolder by default.

Ex: 
command : 
	> ng new my-workspace

output: 

	my-workspace/
	│
	├── src/                ← default app (root app)
	├── angular.json
	├── package.json
	└── tsconfig.json

👉 This first app is placed at the root, not inside projects/.


When you generate additional apps or libraries, must be executed from within a workspace
Command: 
> ng generate application admin
> ng generate application client

output:

	my-workspace/
	│
	├── src/                ← initial app
	├── projects/
	│   ├── admin/
	│   │   └── src/
	│   └── client/
	│       └── src/
	│
	├── angular.json
	└── package.json
	
Advantage : 

Each app:

1) Has its own main.ts
2) Has its own routing
3) Can be deployed separately

	
7) How to run applicatin?
	> ng serve --open

	The "ng serve" command launches the server, watches your files, as well as rebuilds the app and reloads the browser as you make changes to those files.
	The --open (or just -o) option automatically opens your browser to http://localhost:4200/ to view the generated application.	
	
8) What is RouterOutlet?
	> RouterOutlet is a placeholder where Angular injects routed components.
	
	Think of it as:
		“Show the component that matches the current URL here.”	
		
	Example WITH RouterOutlet (✔️)
	
	Routing config
	
	const routes = [
	  { path: 'login', component: LoginComponent },
	  { path: 'dashboard', component: DashboardComponent }
	];	
	
	Template
	
	<nav>
	  <a routerLink="/login">Login</a>
	  <a routerLink="/dashboard">Dashboard</a>
	</nav>

	<router-outlet></router-outlet>

	What happens?
	
	URL				Component rendered
	=====================================
	/login				LoginComponent
	/dashboard		DashboardComponent

    Angular swaps components dynamically inside <router-outlet>.
	
	Why imports: [RouterOutlet]?

		You are using Standalone Components (Angular 14+)

		No "NgModule"

	Every dependency must be imported explicitly

		imports: [RouterOutlet]

	Means:  "This component uses <router-outlet> in its template.”
	
	>>>> Standalone Components vs NgModules using pure Java analogies <<<<
	
	Old Angular (NgModule) → Spring XML / Heavy Configuration
	
	Angular NgModule (OLD)
	================================
	@NgModule({
	  declarations: [AppComponent],
	  imports: [BrowserModule, RouterModule],
	  bootstrap: [AppComponent]
	})
	export class AppModule {}
	
	Java Analogy
	================================
	<beans>
	  <bean id="appComponent" class="com.AppComponent"/>
	  <bean id="router" class="com.Router"/>
	</beans>
	
	or

	@Configuration
	@ComponentScan("com")
	public class AppConfig {}
	
	Problems (same in Java & Angular)

	1) Too much configuration
	2) Indirect wiring
	3) Hard for beginners
	4) “Why is my class not found?” errors
	
	Standalone Components → Spring Boot / Plain Java Class
	
	Angular Standalone Component
	================================
	
	@Component({
	  standalone: true,
	  imports: [RouterOutlet],
	  templateUrl: './app.html'
	})
	export class AppComponent {}


	Java Analogy (Spring Boot)
	================================

	@SpringBootApplication
	public class App {
		public static void main(String[] args) {
			SpringApplication.run(App.class, args);
		}
	}

	👉 No XML
	👉 No extra configuration class
	👉 Everything is self-contained
	
	What does “Standalone” REALLY mean?
	================================
	Java Version
	
	public class Car {
		Engine engine = new Engine();
	}

	Car manages its own dependencies.

	Angular Standalone
	================================
	@Component({
	  imports: [RouterOutlet]
	})

	Component explicitly says:	“I need RouterOutlet”
	
	RouterOutlet java analogy:
	
	RouterOutlet → Controller Dispatcher
	
	Angular
	
		<router-outlet></router-outlet>
		
	Java (Spring MVC)
	================================
	
	@GetMapping("/login")
	public String login() {
		return "login";
	}

	👉 RouterOutlet is like: "DispatcherServlet" deciding which controller/view to show	
	
	
	Full Mental Model (Important)
	
	Java App
	================================
	main()
	 └── loads Spring context
		 └── maps URLs
			 └── returns views

	Angular App
	================================
	main.ts
	 └── bootstrapApplication(AppComponent)
		 └── Router decides URL
			 └── RouterOutlet shows Component
			 
			 
9) How to install typescript compiler? (Pre-requisite- installed NPM)
	> npm install -g typescript
	
10) Running TypeScript
	
	Method 1: Using the Built-in Compiler (tsc)
	
	1) Initialize Project: 
		Open your project folder in VS Code and run "npm init -y" in the terminal to create a package.json file. 	
		
	2) Generate tsconfig.json: Run "tsc --init" to create a tsconfig.json file, which configures the TypeScript compiler.
	
		Write Code: Create your TypeScript file (e.g., src/index.ts).
		
	3) Compile: 
		In the terminal, run tsc (if you configured input/output directories in tsconfig.json) or tsc index.ts. This will generate a JavaScript file (e.g., build/index.js or index.js).
		
	4) Run: Execute the compiled JavaScript file using Node.js:

		node build/index.js	
	
	Method 2: Using ts-node (Direct Execution)
	
		ts-node is a convenient package that runs TypeScript files directly without a separate, manual compilation step. 
	
	1) Install ts-node: Install "ts-node" (globally or as a dev dependency):

		> npm install -g ts-node
	
	2) Run Directly: Execute your TypeScript file using the "ts-node" command:
	 
		> ts-node index.ts
